---json
{
  "layout": "layouts/simulator.njk",
  "title": "Own-data Safety Stock Simulator — Practical Supply Chain Planning",
  "description": "An illustrative teaching simulation for up to 10 SKUs, using your forecast, stock, and prices. It runs in the browser and stores nothing.",
  "canonical": "https://practicalsupplychainplanning.com/learn/safety-stock-simulator/own-data/",
  "nav": "learn",
  "permalink": "/learn/safety-stock-simulator/own-data/",
  "scripts": [
    "/assets/own-data-engine.js?v=15",
    "/assets/sim-results-form.js?v=8",
    "/assets/own-data-simulator.js?v=9"
  ]
}
---
<h1 class="page-title">Own-data Safety Stock Simulator</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        Illustrative estimates for up to 10 SKUs. One average weekly forecast,
        your stock, and your prices.
      </p>

      <div class="sim-app" id="own-app">
        <div id="own-errors" class="sim-error" hidden></div>

        <section class="card">
          <h2 class="card__title">Illustrative estimate</h2>
          <p class="sim-badge">Illustrative estimate</p>
          <div class="content">
            <p>
              These numbers are an illustrative estimate from a teaching
              simulation of the scenario you ran. They are not a forecast, a
              recommendation, or advice for your business.
            </p>
            <p>The estimate uses three assumptions:</p>
            <ul>
              <li>A single average weekly forecast stands in for the whole year.</li>
              <li>Demand, lead time, and delivery quantity move inside fixed variability bands.</li>
              <li>Each week's forecast is split evenly across seven days. Once a week the plan is rebuilt from the forecast and from orders already released. A new receipt cannot arrive until the full lead time has passed, and the plan keeps the normal lot quantity until the goods actually arrive.</li>
            </ul>
            <p id="own-storage-off">
              The calculation runs in this browser. SKU names, forecasts, stock,
              and prices are not stored and are not sent to a server. After you
              edit, the page address holds those numbers after the #, in a
              #d= fragment. That part of the address stays on your device.
              Anyone with the link can read the numbers.
            </p>
            <p id="own-storage-on" hidden>
              SKU names, forecasts, stock, and prices stay in this browser and
              in the #d= part of the link. They are not stored on a server. If
              you ask to email yourself the results, the email contains a
              summary and a reopen link. Anyone who has that email can read the
              numbers. We do not keep the summary or the link.
            </p>
            <p>
              Practical Stock Planner is a separate Windows app that plans stock by weeks
              of cover. It does not calculate safety stock. This page is not a
              cut-down version of that app.
            </p>
            <p>
              The teaching example, with its fixed prices, is still at the
              <a href="/learn/safety-stock-simulator/">safety stock simulator</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <label class="sim-check" for="sim-explanations">
            <input id="sim-explanations" type="checkbox" />
            Show explanations
          </label>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="own-mode-band">
          <h2 class="card__title">Mode</h2>
          <div class="sim-mode-choices" role="group" aria-label="One year or Monte Carlo">
            <button type="button" id="own-mode-year" class="sim-scenario sim-scenario--selected" aria-pressed="true">
              <span class="sim-scenario__name">One year</span>
              <span class="sim-scenario__summary">One 52-week year, with the weekly table and chart.</span>
            </button>
            <button type="button" id="own-mode-mc" class="sim-scenario" aria-pressed="false">
              <span class="sim-scenario__name">Monte Carlo</span>
              <span class="sim-scenario__summary">The same one-year simulation run 50 times with these settings. Summary only, no chart.</span>
            </button>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="own-skus">
          <h2 class="card__title">SKUs</h2>
          <p class="sim-meta" id="own-count"></p>
          <div id="own-sku-bar" class="sim-sku-bar" role="group" aria-label="SKU selector"></div>
          <div class="sim-actions">
            <button type="button" id="own-add" class="sim-button sim-button--secondary">Add SKU</button>
            <button type="button" id="own-remove" class="sim-button sim-button--secondary">Remove SKU</button>
            <button type="button" id="own-copy" class="sim-button sim-button--secondary">Copy link</button>
          </div>
          <p id="own-copy-note" class="sim-meta" hidden></p>
          <div id="own-warnings" class="sim-warning" hidden></div>
          <p class="sim-help">
            Up to 10 SKUs. Delivery quantity and the demand shock are shared.
            Everything else belongs to the SKU selected above. Switching SKU
            does not start a run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="card__title">Paste from a spreadsheet</h3>
          <p>
            Paste tab- or comma-separated rows, including a header. Preview the
            rows before they replace the SKUs on this page.
            <a href="/assets/simulator-example.csv" download>Download an example</a>.
          </p>
          <label class="sim-field" for="own-paste">
            Spreadsheet rows
            <textarea id="own-paste" class="own-paste" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="sku, weekly_forecast, lead_time_weeks, current_stock, unit_cost, selling_price"></textarea>
          </label>
          <div class="sim-actions">
            <button type="button" id="own-preview-btn" class="sim-button sim-button--secondary">Preview paste</button>
          </div>
          <div id="own-preview" hidden>
            <div class="sim-table-wrap">
              <table class="sim-table">
                <caption>Preview. Nothing is applied until you use these SKUs.</caption>
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Row</th>
                    <th scope="col">SKU</th>
                    <th scope="col">Weekly forecast</th>
                    <th scope="col">Lead time</th>
                    <th scope="col">Current stock</th>
                    <th scope="col">Unit cost</th>
                    <th scope="col">Selling price</th>
                    <th scope="col">Check</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody id="own-preview-body"></tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <p id="own-preview-note" class="sim-meta"></p>
            <button type="button" id="own-apply" class="sim-button" disabled>Use these SKUs</button>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="card">
          <h2 class="card__title">This SKU</h2>
          <div class="sim-grid">
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-label">
              SKU name
              <input id="own-label" type="text" maxlength="40" autocomplete="off" value="SKU 1" />
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-forecast">
              Weekly forecast
              <input id="own-forecast" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="40" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-stock">
              Current stock
              <input id="own-stock" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="80" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-cost">
              Unit cost
              <input id="own-cost" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="70" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-price">
              Selling price
              <input id="own-price" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
          </div>
          <p class="sim-help">
            The weekly forecast is one average for all 52 weeks. It replaces the
            Level, Seasonal, and Rising patterns on the teaching page. Current
            stock is the opening balance. It is not calculated from the forecast.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card">
          <h2 class="card__title">Replenishment</h2>
          <div class="sim-grid">
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-lot-mode">
              Lot size
              <select id="own-lot-mode">
                <option value="fixed">Fixed quantity</option>
                <option value="weeks">Weeks cover</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" id="own-lot-qty-field" for="own-lot-qty">
              Fixed lot quantity
              <input id="own-lot-qty" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="40" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" id="own-lot-weeks-field" for="own-lot-weeks" hidden>
              Weeks of cover
              <select id="own-lot-weeks">
                <option value="2">2</option>
                <option value="4" selected>4</option>
                <option value="6">6</option>
                <option value="10">10</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-ss-mode">
              Safety stock
              <select id="own-ss-mode">
                <option value="fixed">Fixed quantity</option>
                <option value="formula">Formula</option>
                <option value="weeks">Weeks cover</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" id="own-ss-qty-field" for="own-ss-qty">
              Fixed safety stock
              <input id="own-ss-qty" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="20" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" id="own-ss-weeks-field" for="own-ss-weeks" hidden>
              Safety stock cover
              <select id="own-ss-weeks">
                <option value="2">2</option>
                <option value="4" selected>4</option>
                <option value="6">6</option>
                <option value="10">10</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" id="own-service-field" for="own-service" hidden>
              Formula service level
              <select id="own-service">
                <option value="90">90%</option>
                <option value="95" selected>95%</option>
                <option value="98">98%</option>
                <option value="99">99%</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-lead">
              Lead time (weeks)
              <input id="own-lead" type="number" min="1" max="26" step="1" value="5" autocomplete="off" />
            </label>
          </div>
          <p id="own-formula-note" class="sim-meta" hidden></p>
          <p class="sim-help">
            Each week's forecast is split evenly across seven days, and those
            days add up to the week. A receipt due on a day is in stock
            before that day's demand. A day with no receipt takes demand
            off the opening stock. At the start of
            each week the plan is rebuilt from the forecast and from orders
            already released, counted on the date they are still expected. A
            new receipt cannot arrive until the full lead time has passed.
            An order released in week 1 with a 5-week lead time is due on the
            first day of week 6, before that day's demand. Until the goods
            arrive the plan keeps the normal lot quantity. A miss is
            recognised when that day passes with no receipt. The plan then
            expects that same quantity one week later, and moves the
            expectation forward again if the goods still have not arrived.
            It does not learn the actual arrival date in advance. A short
            shipment is recognised only when the goods arrive. The release is
            as many normal lots as the forecast needs to finish the week at
            or above safety stock. An order already on the way is counted on
            its expected date, and another lot can be due that same week if
            the forecast still finishes below safety stock. Lead time is any
            whole number of weeks from 1 to 26.
            Lead-time variability adds whole extra weeks after that lead time
            and cannot bring the receipt forward.
          </p>
          <p class="sim-help">
            Formula safety stock is round(Z × σD × √L). Z comes from the
            service level you pick: a higher service level uses a bigger Z,
            so the buffer is larger. σD is how far weekly demand usually sits
            from the forecast. L is the lead time in weeks. Multiplying by
            the square root of L spreads that weekly variation over the weeks
            you wait for an order. The result is extra stock on top of the
            forecast. It assumes a fixed lead time and random demand only.
            With demand variability off and a flat forecast, σ is 0, so the
            formula result is 0.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card">
          <h2 class="card__title">Variability</h2>
          <div class="sim-grid">
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-demand-var">
              Demand for this SKU
              <select id="own-demand-var">
                <option value="off">Off</option>
                <option value="small">Small</option>
                <option value="medium" selected>Medium</option>
                <option value="large">Large</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-lt-var">
              Lead time for this SKU
              <select id="own-lt-var">
                <option value="off" selected>Off</option>
                <option value="small">Small</option>
                <option value="medium">Medium</option>
                <option value="large">Large</option>
              </select>
            </label>
            <label class="sim-field" for="own-delivery-var">
              Delivery quantity for every SKU
              <select id="own-delivery-var">
                <option value="off" selected>Off</option>
                <option value="small">Small</option>
                <option value="medium">Medium</option>
                <option value="large">Large</option>
              </select>
            </label>
          </div>
          <p class="sim-help">
            Demand variability defaults to Medium. Off uses the weekly forecast
            as the demand. Small and Medium stay nearer the forecast. Large is
            the extreme band, from none of the weekly forecast up to double it.
            The forecast is the expected mean. Across many runs those weeks
            average to the forecast. One year's realized mean can sit above or
            below it. These are the same bands as the teaching simulator. The
            draw is for the whole week, then that week is split evenly across
            its seven days. Delivery
            quantity is shared by every SKU.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="own-demand-shock">
          <h2 class="card__title">Demand shock</h2>
          <div class="sim-shock-run">
            <div class="sim-shock-run__choice">
              <label class="sim-check" for="own-shock">
                <input id="own-shock" type="checkbox" />
                Double three demand weeks
              </label>
              <p id="own-shock-weeks" class="sim-meta"></p>
            </div>
            <div class="sim-actions">
              <button id="own-run-year" class="sim-button" type="button">Run year</button>
              <button id="own-run-mc" class="sim-button" type="button" hidden>Run Monte Carlo</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p id="own-linked" class="sim-meta" hidden>Press Run to see this scenario.</p>
          <p id="own-progress" class="sim-meta" hidden></p>
          <p class="sim-help">
            The shock is shared by every SKU. When it is on, three weeks from
            week 21 to week 52 are doubled. Those weeks are chosen from the
            sample number, at least four weeks apart.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card sim-panel" id="own-year-panel" aria-live="polite">
          <h2 class="card__title">One simulated year</h2>
          <p id="own-year-empty" class="sim-meta">
            No year yet. Press Run year at the end of the settings.
          </p>
          <div id="own-year-output" hidden>
            <p class="sim-badge">Illustrative estimate</p>
            <p id="own-status" class="sim-meta"></p>
            <dl class="sim-params" id="own-snapshot"></dl>
            <p id="own-callout" class="sim-callout" hidden></p>
            <div id="own-commentary" class="own-commentary"></div>
            <div class="sim-metrics" id="own-metrics"></div>
            <p class="sim-note">
              Annual gross profit counts only units actually sold. Demand is
              filled from the stock available that morning, after a receipt
              due that day. Stock that is already below zero before the
              receipt is still a stockout. Inventory turns and working
              capital use the daily points on the chart. A plotted point
              below zero counts as zero, so working capital is not negative.
              The table stays one row per week. A week is out of stock if
              stock is below zero before a receipt due that morning, or
              after demand when nothing arrives.
            </p>
            <figure class="sim-figure">
              <svg id="own-chart" class="sim-chart" role="img" aria-label="Stock on hand, safety stock, and zero"></svg>
              <figcaption>
                <ul class="sim-legend">
                  <li class="sim-legend__ending">Stock on hand</li>
                  <li class="sim-legend__ss">Safety stock</li>
                  <li class="sim-legend__zero">Zero</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="sim-meta">
                  One year, one point per day: the stock after that day's demand.
                  A receipt day also shows the stock just before the receipt.
                  Monte Carlo does not draw this chart.
                </p>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p class="sim-help">
              SOH means stock on hand, the units in stock. The table is one
              row per week. Beginning SOH is the stock at the start of the
              week. Pre-receipt SOH is the lowest stock before a receipt is
              added: the morning stock on a receipt day, and the stock after
              demand on every other day. It is not the lowest close after a
              receipt. Ending SOH is the stock at the end of the week. A week
              is out of stock if stock is below zero before a receipt due that
              morning, or after demand. Touching zero is not a stockout.
            </p>
            <details class="sim-details">
              <summary>Weekly table</summary>
              <div class="sim-table-wrap sim-scroll--weeks">
                <table class="sim-table">
                  <caption>Weekly results for this SKU. Illustrative estimate.</caption>
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Week</th>
                      <th scope="col">Base demand</th>
                      <th scope="col">Simulated demand</th>
                      <th scope="col">Beginning SOH</th>
                      <th scope="col">Pre-receipt SOH</th>
                      <th scope="col">Receipts</th>
                      <th scope="col">Ending SOH</th>
                      <th scope="col">Safety stock</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody id="own-weeks-body"></tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              <p class="sim-actions">
                <button id="own-download-weeks" class="sim-button sim-button--secondary" type="button">Download weekly CSV</button>
              </p>
            </details>
            <p class="sim-note">
              These numbers are an illustrative estimate from a teaching
              simulation of the scenario you ran. They are not a forecast, a
              recommendation, or advice for your business.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="card sim-panel" id="own-mc-panel" hidden>
          <h2 class="card__title">Monte Carlo</h2>
          <p id="own-mc-empty" class="sim-meta">
            No Monte Carlo batch yet. Press Run Monte Carlo at the end of the settings.
          </p>
          <div id="own-mc-results" hidden>
            <p class="sim-badge">Illustrative estimate</p>
            <p id="own-mc-callout" class="sim-callout" hidden></p>
            <div id="own-mc-commentary" class="own-commentary"></div>
            <div class="sim-metrics" id="own-mc-card"></div>
            <p class="sim-note">
              Out-of-stock weeks, customer service, inventory turns, and
              average working capital show the median and the P10–P90 band.
              Annual gross profit (units sold) shows the median only. It
              counts only units actually sold. Demand that the morning stock,
              after a receipt due that day, could not fill is left out.
            </p>
            <details class="sim-details">
              <summary>Per-run results</summary>
              <div class="sim-table-wrap sim-scroll--weeks">
                <table class="sim-table">
                  <caption>One row per retained year. There is no Monte Carlo chart.</caption>
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Run</th>
                      <th scope="col">OOS weeks</th>
                      <th scope="col">Customer service</th>
                      <th scope="col">Inventory turns</th>
                      <th scope="col">Average working capital</th>
                      <th scope="col">Annual gross profit (units sold)</th>
                      <th scope="col">Safety stock</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody id="own-mc-body"></tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </details>
            <p class="sim-note">
              These numbers are an illustrative estimate from a teaching
              simulation of the scenario you ran. They are not a forecast, a
              recommendation, or advice for your business.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="sim-help">
            P10–P90 is the likely range. In 8 out of 10 simulated years, the
            result falls inside it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="card" id="own-summary">
          <h2 class="card__title">All SKUs</h2>
          <p class="sim-badge">Illustrative estimate</p>
          <p id="own-summary-empty" class="sim-meta">
            No results yet. Press Run year or Run Monte Carlo.
          </p>
          <div id="own-summary-wrap" class="sim-table-wrap" hidden>
            <table class="sim-table">
              <caption id="own-summary-caption">
                One row per SKU. The total adds working capital and gross profit only.
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">SKU</th>
                  <th scope="col">Out-of-stock weeks</th>
                  <th scope="col">Customer service level</th>
                  <th scope="col">Inventory turns</th>
                  <th scope="col">Average working capital</th>
                  <th scope="col">Annual gross profit (units sold)</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody id="own-summary-body"></tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="sim-help">
            In Monte Carlo, the table shows medians. P10–P90 is the likely
            range: in 8 out of 10 simulated years, the result falls inside
            it. The total adds those medians. It is not the median of one
            combined year. Out-of-stock weeks, customer service, and turns
            are not added across SKUs. Annual gross profit counts only units
            actually sold.
          </p>
        </section>

        {% set resultsId = "own-results-email" %}
        {% include "partials/sim-results-form.njk" %}
      </div>
